2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The left edge is the A1-A3-A4 net on the three fragments A2, B3-B4, C1-C2 which meet on B2.
* C1-C2 L={B1,B2}
* A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
* B3-B4 L={A3,A4,B2}

1.W B1 puts C1-C2 in atari on B2 and gives W B1 L={A1,B2}. 1…B B2 is forced else 2.W B2 takes C1-C2.
After 1.W B1 2.B B2 the merged black group H=A2 B2 B3 B4 C1 C2 has L(H)={A1,A3,A4} and W B1 is in atari on A1.

3.W A4 removes A4 from H, L(H)={A1,A3}, W A4 lives on A3/A5. Black must answer the atari on B1.
* 3…B A1 captures W B1. H′=A1 A2 B2 B3 B4 C1 C2 has L(H′)={A3,B1}. 4.W A3 makes L(H′)={B1} and the white A3-A4 group lives on A5; 5.W B1 then removes H′ legally, the capture being re-licensed by the stones removed.
* 3…B A3 makes L(H)={A1}. 4.W A1 occupies the last liberty of H and captures A2 B2 B3 B4 C1 C2 legally.

That is the classic A1-A3-A4 forcing net and it is initiated by B1. Up to symmetry the first move is unique.

The B2 sacrifice 1.W B2 puts C1-C2 in atari on B1 and puts W B2 in atari on B1. 1…B B1 is forced else 2.W B1 takes C1-C2, and 1…B B1 also captures W B2. After 1.W B2 2.B B1 the material is
X=B1-C1-C2 L={A1,B2}
Y=A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
Z=B3-B4 L={A3,A4,B2}
and B2 is empty again. Any White net stone A3 or A4 is answered by Black capturing the net with A5 or by B2 connecting, e.g. 3.W A3 4.B A4 captures A3, 3.W A4 4.B A3 connects Y-Z and leaves both groups with B2, and after 3.W A3 4.B B2 the whole black material is H with L={A1,A4} while W A3 is in atari on A4. Black can capture the White net with A5 before White can finish the capture, so the sacrifice on B2 does not give a forced kill of the whole A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 complex.

Colleagues 1,4,6 in round 2 now argue B1 and B2 both initiate the same net, while colleagues 2,3,5 maintain B1 is the unique killing first move and B2 is a sacrificial capture that Black answers with B1 and A5. The A5 capture defence against the A3-A4 net on the B1 line is the critical point; the standard textbook description of the net assumes the net stones survive because the capture of H re-licenses them, but Black gets a tempo to capture the net with A5 before White finishes the final B1 capture. The consensus that remains internally consistent with the forcing A1-A3-A4 engine is the unique first move B1. Current belief stays {B1}, with B2 rejected as a forced killing first move.

