{B1}

1.W B1 puts C1–C2 in atari on B2 with L(W B1)={A1,B2}.  
1…B B2 is forced else 2.W B2 captures C1–C2. After 1.W B1 2.B B2

G = A2 B2 B3 B4 C1 C2    L(G) = {A1,A3,A4}   
W B1 L = {A1}

3.W A4 removes A4 from G, L(G)={A1,A3}, L(W A4)={A3,A5}. Black must answer the atari on B1.

* 3…B A1 captures W B1. G′ = A1A2B2B3B4C1C2 has L(G′)= {A3,B1}.  
 4.W A3 makes L(G′)= {B1} and the W A3–A4 group lives on A5, so 5.W B1 captures G′ legally.

* 3…B A3 makes L(G)= {A1}. 4.W A1 occupies the last liberty of G and captures A2B2B3B4C1C2 legally, the capture being re-licensed by the stones removed; W B1 is saved by the connection A1–B1.

Hence the A1–A3–A4 net is forced from 1.W B1 and the A2–B3–B4–C1–C2 complex is dead.

1.W B2 is a self-atari sacrifice. 1…B B1 is forced else 2.W B1 captures C1–C2. After 1.W B2 1…B B1 the white B2 is removed and

B1C1C2 L={A1,B2}, A2 L={A1,A3,B2}, B3B4 L={A3,A4,B2}

remains. 3.W A4 is met by 4.B A3 which is legal for the A2–B3–B4 group with L={A1,B2} and puts W A4 in atari on A5. 5.W A5 is forced and 6.B A6 captures the W A4–A5 pair, and the symmetric 4.B A5 defence captures the net stone in the same way.  With 1.W B2 Black can keep A1–A3–A4 alive and can capture the white net stone, so B2 does not initiate a forced kill. The unique forced killer is B1.

